--- T2  the same at deliberately REDUCED rank ---
  "Reduced rank" has to mean a GENUINELY rank-deficient basis, one with a zero
  singular value, which is what the any-rank proposition of the derivation is about.
  Rank deficiency then costs only the uniqueness of the coefficient. Truncating a
  NONZERO singular value is a different operation, measured separately below.

  (a) GENUINE deficiency, a duplicated column (span unchanged, one sigma = 0):
    basis                                rank      residual    norm(G~)/norm(G)
    full [J | c]                           11    9.9099e-17              0.9731
    column 3 duplicated                    11    7.2071e-17              0.9731
    columns 3 and 7 duplicated             11    1.2012e-16              0.9731
    a column zeroed                        10    6.0964e-18              0.9737
  genuine rank deficiency, residual 7.2071e-17   MET (<= 1e-12)

  (b) ARTIFICIAL truncation of NONZERO singular values, for the record:
         rcond   rank      residual    norm(G~)/norm(G)
         1e-10     11    9.9099e-17              0.9731
         1e-02     11    9.9099e-17              0.9731
         1e-01     11    9.9099e-17              0.9731
         3e-01      9    2.0261e-04              0.9737
         6e-01      8    1.3097e-02              0.9742
  Truncating a nonzero sigma_i leaves exactly that direction in the residual, so
  exactness is LOST, not preserved. The any-rank guarantee covers genuine deficiency
  only. That is an argument for the column scaling of stage 2, which is what keeps a
  sigma_0-relative cutoff from discarding genuine directions in the first place.

